{:edb/db-before-t 2058 :edb/db-after-t 2059 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535371 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:00:45.050Z" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157106 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ac922b185c42645f703c13d4f5016941b4125d81e85fa5ff529aa5c8d4010ac8.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x.$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157106 1100 "Find the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/8c6c7cf5762a3078063987d1dc5eb95f.png)" 13194139535371 false] [17592186157106 1145 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0, \\pm a)$. From the graph, we have that $(0,9)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=9$.\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny = \\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny = \\pm 3 x\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a}{b} &= 3 \\\\\n\\frac{9}{b} &= 3 \\\\\nb &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\tan{t},\\quad y=9\\sec{t}\n$$" 13194139535371 false] [17592186157106 1145 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0, \\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,9)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=9.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola are $y = \\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y = \\pm 3 x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{b} & =3 \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\tan{t},\\quad y=9\\sec{t}.\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157107 1100 "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{y^2}{9} - \\dfrac{x^2}{25} = 1$?" 13194139535371 false] [17592186157107 1100 "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{y^2}{9} - \\dfrac{x^2}{25} = 1?$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157107 1145 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx = b\\tan{t}, \\quad y = a\\sec{t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nb = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx = 5\\tan{t}, \\quad y = 3\\sec{t}\n$$" 13194139535371 false] [17592186157107 1145 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx = b\\tan{t}, \\quad y = a\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a = \\sqrt{9} = 3$ and $b = \\sqrt{25} = 5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx = 5\\tan{t}, \\quad y = 3\\sec{t}.\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157108 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x =2\\tan{t}, \\, y =3\\sec{t}$." 13194139535371 false] [17592186157108 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x =2\\tan{t}, \\, y =3\\sec{t}.$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157108 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t$. We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n$x|= 2\\tan t; \\frac{x}{2}|= \\tan t; \\frac{x^{2}}{2^{2}}|= \\tan^{2} t; \\frac{x^{2}}{4}|= \\tan^{2} t$.\n- Isolating the $\\sec{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n$y|= 3\\sec t; \\frac{y}{3}|= \\sec t; \\frac{y^{2}}{3^{2}}|= \\sec^{2} t; \\frac{y^{2}}{9}|= \\sec^{2} t$.\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}\n$$\n\nand rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan^{2} t + 1 &= \\sec^{2} t \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + 1 &= \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{9} - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535371 false] [17592186157108 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157124 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/2944e095d71b64fb2f9e6e0121a09fb9eb2f3ffe765f9881162c95fe6875a831.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157124 1100 "The quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/b31abfb21d7c23e3c4f5c2a1ed1eb344.png)\n\n$x = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?" 13194139535371 false] [17592186157124 1145 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad\nt \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad\nt \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/b1d78a939f85c533bcd9b4609346f2a15385aad3a6b074a62b791551cf784418.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, 0\\right]$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad\nt \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,0\\right].\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157124 1145 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad t \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad t \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/ff70cc02658a737d406109329d5c2ccf.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[MATH: \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, 0\\right]]$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$[MATH: x= \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,0\\right].]$" 13194139535371 false] [17592186157290 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346332 13194139535371 true] [17592186157290 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346339 13194139535371 true] [17592186157290 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346346 13194139535371 true] [17592186157290 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346374 13194139535371 true] [17592186157290 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346391 13194139535371 true] [17592186157291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346325 13194139535371 true] [17592186157291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346382 13194139535371 true] [17592186157291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346385 13194139535371 true] [17592186157291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346388 13194139535371 true] [17592186157292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346353 13194139535371 true] [17592186157292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346360 13194139535371 true] [17592186157292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346379 13194139535371 true] [17592186157292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346396 13194139535371 true] [17592186157292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346406 13194139535371 true] [17592186157305 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346367 13194139535371 true] [17592186157305 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346400 13194139535371 true] [17592186157305 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346413 13194139535371 true] [17592186157905 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535371 true] [17592186157905 1100 "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\frac{y^{2}}{25} - \\frac{x^{2}}{16} = 1$?" 13194139535371 false] [17592186157905 1100 "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}=1?$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157905 1145 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{25}=5$ and $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157935 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535371 true] [17592186157935 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/600981b59a97fd1d63c9da1f7ef45d58c8a2d5dcad1ec05df91a380e0f4e154e.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157935 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/q-90951.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\tan t, y = 2\\sec t$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?" 13194139535371 false] [17592186157935 1145 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/747613184fe04ecd53fb69e6e00c5d91152e571e9bc4db81adeef1063c057491.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the lower branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the lower branch are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157936 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535371 true] [17592186157936 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/7be6e3cc933586287cabf590d2a3c5fede604b6c4222a39c9db78f3a3745f814.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186157936 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/q-91323.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\tan t, y = 3\\sec t$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?" 13194139535371 false] [17592186157936 1145 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ebb0f765fca0bc4e3a0a789800779a51eb4173cf0384b8b3628ac679a5a3d692.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346325 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346326 13194139535371 true] [17592186346325 1089 "q-106608" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346325 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535371 true] [17592186346325 1100 "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $5{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=20?$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346325 1113 #uuid "d04dc5b8-08a6-57d8-bded-d4e9790647ca" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346325 1145 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{5{y}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{20} & =\\frac{20}{20} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, $a=\\sqrt{4}=2$ and $b=\\sqrt{5}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346327 13194139535371 true] [17592186346326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346328 13194139535371 true] [17592186346326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346329 13194139535371 true] [17592186346326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346330 13194139535371 true] [17592186346326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346331 13194139535371 true] [17592186346326 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535371 true] [17592186346326 1041 "selection" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346326 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346328 13194139535371 true] [17592186346326 1110 #uuid "8ae907b9-6df9-547e-86a3-2e805cd0d3e9" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346327 1034 "x=2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346327 1111 #uuid "db0e8dbb-c5db-54e6-bad5-ce5488bccf5e" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346328 1034 "x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346328 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346328 1111 #uuid "a61909ad-7bb4-5f82-9b8b-3750e2579bd5" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346329 1034 "x=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346329 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346329 1111 #uuid "16014427-57f8-522b-b2e0-7942df6e6430" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346330 1034 "x=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346330 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346330 1111 #uuid "c2935083-a9ca-5589-9c3b-f95aabe2b563" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346331 1034 "x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346331 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346331 1111 #uuid "ca798256-17c8-5b28-aa33-9f2402690cb9" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346332 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346333 13194139535371 true] [17592186346332 1089 "q-106615" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346332 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535371 true] [17592186346332 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/09873582f6b52b28ca5223605479cc98df4ead7cf0940ea4cadfa66301694911.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}x.$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346332 1113 #uuid "fabfdbc4-b695-5ce4-bd18-c77c662dc09c" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346332 1145 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,\\sqrt{3})$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{b} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346334 13194139535371 true] [17592186346333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346335 13194139535371 true] [17592186346333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346336 13194139535371 true] [17592186346333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346337 13194139535371 true] [17592186346333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346338 13194139535371 true] [17592186346333 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535371 true] [17592186346333 1041 "selection" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346333 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346338 13194139535371 true] [17592186346333 1110 #uuid "4f87579f-d2ff-556b-9b50-773ad175d759" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346334 1034 "x=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346334 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346334 1111 #uuid "4134906b-ade5-585e-9eed-8a