true] [17592186346391 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535371 true] [17592186346391 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/e2c0bc639d03c94056b07ce1cc42976b88494f312e318199cef73043bc711c74.png)\n\nComplete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola above in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,$\n$y=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346391 1113 #uuid "fab15ed8-d110-5c0a-bb37-2091717ed4f2" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346391 1145 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,3)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=3.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{b} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ b & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346392 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346393 13194139535371 true] [17592186346392 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535371 true] [17592186346392 1041 "field-1" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346392 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346393 13194139535371 true] [17592186346392 1110 #uuid "872d4ecb-c1d8-53f2-87d5-d367cd2be66a" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346393 1034 "\\sqrt{2}" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346393 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346393 1111 #uuid "eb9f22c3-74c4-5b8e-bc81-08a88cfb90cf" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346394 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346395 13194139535371 true] [17592186346394 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535371 true] [17592186346394 1041 "field-2" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346394 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346395 13194139535371 true] [17592186346394 1110 #uuid "d84a12cf-ca2d-55a7-b2c1-bd05ace5fe9b" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346395 1034 "3\\sec(t)" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346395 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346395 1111 #uuid "f2117b3c-2428-544d-9df7-c0c99926ac4d" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346396 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346397 13194139535371 true] [17592186346396 1089 "q-325905" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346396 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535371 true] [17592186346396 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=3\\sqrt{5}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346396 1113 #uuid "ea996889-9e23-5a96-b5ed-4f94b6d927c3" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346396 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant–tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{5}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{3\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{45} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{24} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{45}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{45} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346397 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346398 13194139535371 true] [17592186346397 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346399 13194139535371 true] [17592186346397 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535371 true] [17592186346397 1041 "field-1" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346397 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346398 13194139535371 true] [17592186346397 1110 #uuid "8b0f1d42-9a59-58e8-b31c-57af64087727" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346398 1034 "\\frac{y^2}{24}-\\frac{x^2}{45}" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346398 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346398 1111 #uuid "7ee5f6e0-89ce-5954-98b4-0653b10a808a" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346399 1034 "\\frac{y^2}{24}+\\frac{x^2}{45}" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346399 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346399 1111 #uuid "f3958474-4370-572d-8659-d028ef6cac29" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346400 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346401 13194139535371 true] [17592186346400 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346404 13194139535371 true] [17592186346400 1089 "q-326334" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346400 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535371 true] [17592186346400 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/28c189efc805ec6aef299c4a89c1bb9299db7ad9f8544b86ed7509e6abc2e6a5.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this branch, assuming that the entire hyperbola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346400 1113 #uuid "41eceb87-fa62-57cc-85ca-797ce60afd1b" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346400 1145 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/b8b14a6d9bf2f77c79b26172ec18e7833c1315ccb09c94be5a05b7e12714a14f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346402 13194139535371 true] [17592186346401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346403 13194139535371 true] [17592186346401 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535371 true] [17592186346401 1041 "field-1" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346401 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346402 13194139535371 true] [17592186346401 1110 #uuid "264d09be-01e6-51cb-8336-7f9cefe6c971" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346402 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346402 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346402 1111 #uuid "dae80fb9-5c18-57b5-b4c9-82cebb514975" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346403 1034 "-\\pi" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346403 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346403 1111 #uuid "de67c31e-a563-5e0e-8ba9-590a5dd6ccad" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346404 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346405 13194139535371 true] [17592186346404 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535371 true] [17592186346404 1041 "field-2" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346404 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346405 13194139535371 true] [17592186346404 1110 #uuid "f1b997d0-94ec-5723-916d-f0d7431121d4" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346405 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346405 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535371 true] [17592186346405 1111 #uuid "e5bd24f6-6d6e-535e-b32c-a9d04c9158b6" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346406 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346407 13194139535371 true] [17592186346406 1089 "q-89778" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346406 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535371 true] [17592186346406 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346406 1113 #uuid "f67d78d3-8bb9-5eaa-a448-9f4613ae0ed6" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346406 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Squaring the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{2}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-{x}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346408 13194139535371 true] [17592186346407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346409 13194139535371 true] [17592186346407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346410 13194139535371 true] [17592186346407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346411 13194139535371 true] [17592186346407 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346412 13194139535371 true] [17592186346407 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535371 true] [17592186346407 1041 "selection" 13194139535371 true] [17592186346407 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346411 13194139535371 true] [17592186346407 1110 #uuid "4ce7